{"type":"finding","severity":"minor","fileName":"src/bioetl/domain/behavior/organism_classification_service_models.py","codegenInstructions":"Verify each finding against current code. Fix only still-valid issues, skip the rest with a brief reason, keep changes minimal, and validate.\n\nIn @src/bioetl/domain/behavior/organism_classification_service_models.py around lines 41 - 61, Update ClassificationStats.__post_init__ to reject conflict_count values greater than total, while preserving the existing non-negative and bucket-sum validations. Ensure valid counts remain deterministic and enforce that conflict_count is bounded by total.","suggestions":["    def __post_init__(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Reject negative counters and inconsistent bucket totals.\"\"\"\n        for name in (\n            \"total\",\n            \"acellular\",\n            \"unicellular\",\n            \"multicellular\",\n            \"unresolved\",\n            \"conflict_count\",\n        ):\n            value = getattr(self, name)\n            if value < 0:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{name} cannot be negative\")\n        bucket_sum = (\n            self.acellular + self.unicellular + self.multicellular + self.unresolved\n        )\n        if bucket_sum != self.total:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"cellularity bucket counts must sum to total \"\n                f\"(got {bucket_sum} != {self.total})\"\n            )\n        if self.conflict_count > self.total:\n            raise ValueError(\n                f\"conflict_count cannot exceed total \"\n                f\"(got {self.conflict_count} > {self.total})\"\n            )"]}
{"type":"finding","severity":"minor","fileName":"src/bioetl/domain/behavior/schema_classifier_helpers.py","codegenInstructions":"Verify each finding against current code. Fix only still-valid issues, skip the rest with a brief reason, keep changes minimal, and validate.\n\nIn @src/bioetl/domain/behavior/schema_classifier_helpers.py around lines 68 - 89, Update removed_field_changes and added_field_changes to iterate over sorted field names before building SchemaChange objects. Preserve the existing membership checks and value lookups while ensuring both returned lists have deterministic ordering consistent with changed_field_changes.","suggestions":["def removed_field_changes(\n    old_properties: JsonDict,\n    new_properties: JsonDict,\n) -> list[SchemaChange]:\n    \"\"\"Return field removal changes.\"\"\"\n    return [\n        build_field_removed_change(field_name, old_properties[field_name])\n        for field_name in sorted(old_properties)\n        if field_name not in new_properties\n    ]\n\n\ndef added_field_changes(\n    old_properties: JsonDict,\n    new_properties: JsonDict,\n) -> list[SchemaChange]:\n    \"\"\"Return field addition changes.\"\"\"\n    return [\n        build_field_added_change(field_name, new_properties[field_name])\n        for field_name in sorted(new_properties)\n        if field_name not in old_properties\n    ]"]}
{"type":"finding","severity":"trivial","fileName":"src/bioetl/domain/behavior/text_similarity.py","codegenInstructions":"Verify each finding against current code. Fix only still-valid issues, skip the rest with a brief reason, keep changes minimal, and validate.\n\nIn @src/bioetl/domain/behavior/text_similarity.py around lines 39 - 58, Update jaccard_similarity’s docstring to state that it returns 0.0 when either normalized text produces no words, including punctuation-only input. Remove the unnecessary union > 0 conditional from the final return, since the earlier empty-set check guarantees a nonzero union.","suggestions":["def jaccard_similarity(text_a: str, text_b: str) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Compute word-level Jaccard similarity between two texts.\n\n    Normalizes both texts, splits into word sets, and computes\n    |intersection| / |union|.\n\n    Args:\n        text_a: First text.\n        text_b: Second text.\n\n    Returns:\n        Jaccard similarity in [0.0, 1.0]. Returns 0.0 if either text is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    a_words = set(normalize_text(text_a).split())\n    b_words = set(normalize_text(text_b).split())\n    if not a_words or not b_words:\n        return 0.0\n    return len(a_words & b_words) / len(a_words | b_words)"]}
{"type":"finding","severity":"major","fileName":"src/bioetl/domain/behavior/schema_classifier.py","codegenInstructions":"Verify each finding against current code. Fix only still-valid issues, skip the rest with a brief reason, keep changes minimal, and validate.\n\nIn @src/bioetl/domain/behavior/schema_classifier.py around lines 133 - 144, Update classify_from_registry_diff to validate that both decoded JSON payloads are objects before calling classify_changes; route non-object values such as arrays, null, and numbers to the existing manual_review fallback without allowing AttributeError to escape. Add a unit test covering classify_from_registry_diff(\"[]\", \"{}\") and assert the manual-review classification.","suggestions":["    def classify_from_registry_diff(\n        self, old_schema_str: str, new_schema_str: str\n    ) -> SchemaChangeClassification:\n        \"\"\"Classify changes from string representations.\"\"\"\n        try:\n            old_schema = json.loads(old_schema_str)\n            new_schema = json.loads(new_schema_str)\n        except json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n            return SchemaChangeClassification.manual_review(\n                explanation=f\"Invalid JSON: {e!s}\"\n            )\n        if not isinstance(old_schema, dict) or not isinstance(new_schema, dict):\n            return SchemaChangeClassification.manual_review(\n                explanation=\"Schema payloads must be JSON objects\"\n            )\n        return self.classify_changes(old_schema, new_schema)"]}
{"type":"finding","severity":"major","fileName":"src/bioetl/domain/behavior/normalization_service.py","codegenInstructions":"Verify each finding against current code. Fix only still-valid issues, skip the rest with a brief reason, keep changes minimal, and validate.\n\nIn @src/bioetl/domain/behavior/normalization_service.py around lines 148 - 158, Update NormalizationService.classify_potency to use a single consistent boundary scheme derived from NormalizationConfig, eliminating the hardcoded 4.0 and 6.0 cutoffs so results agree with is_potent and configured thresholds. Add meaningful unit tests covering potency boundaries for all three factory configurations, including exact threshold values and values just below or above them.","suggestions":["    def classify_potency(self, pchembl_value: float) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Classify potency level based on pChEMBL value.\"\"\"\n        potent = self.config.potency_threshold\n        highly_potent = self.config.high_potency_threshold\n        moderate = (potent + highly_potent) / 2\n        if pchembl_value < potent - 1.0:\n            return \"inactive\"\n        if pchembl_value < potent:\n            return \"weak\"\n        if pchembl_value < moderate:\n            return \"moderate\"\n        if pchembl_value < highly_potent:\n            return \"potent\"\n        return \"highly_potent\""]}
